    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Even if there are no peanuts in the ingredients, the body's reaction to allergens can't always be predicted solely based on labels. Some reactions can be caused by substances that seemed unrelated. Also, while caffeine may help focus and alleviate some of the nervousness, it can also increase heart rate, especially when it combines with an underlying allergic response and pre-existing nervousness. Given your current symptoms and concerns, it's safer to choose caution over convenience right now. Engaging in your workout might relieve stress, but if an allergic reaction occurs, it could become dangerous and potentially interaction with the more potent ingredients in your pre-workout. Discuss taking the pre-workout for now; reassuringly, it appears to contain no peanuts, but side effects from caffeine might still lead to an increase in your heart rate. A few minutes of waiting could contextualize your body's response to both stressors; if everything seems normal, then taking your pre-workout seems safe. However, to be entirely certain, consider skipping it until physical symptoms subside or speak with a healthcare professional. Your health and safety are paramount, and it's better to err on the side of caution, ensuring your gym session is blissfully successful without compromising your well-being.",
